reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
The only random element is which card is removed after a failed challenge. A failed challenge needs a wrong read or a forced all-zero start. The removal mainly matters through whether the challenger loses the Skull, roughly a 1/4 to 1/3 chance per failure. That changes their future threat, but it never directly awards marks.

Marks come entirely from choices:
- inferring who placed a Skull from talk, bids and past reveals;
- tracking who probably still holds a Skull from Skulls revealed in earlier hands and card counts;
- bluffing bids and talk;
- choosing targets.

When everyone plays well, players mix placement and bluffing. Who wins then depends on reading opponents across about 4 to 10 hands, not on a deal. There is no hidden type or deal that decides the game. I estimate chance at about 20 to 30% of the result. A good reasoner, for example one who targets observed Rose placers and avoids players with a revealed Skull still in hand, is clearly favoured over a random player.
